mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p>Emergency nursing is a vital component of nursing science, emerging as a new clinical discipline focused on the rescue, monitoring, and care of critically ill and injured patients. This course is divided into three major parts: pre-hospital emergency care, in-hospital emergency care, and intensive care. Its main content covers common clinical acute and critical conditions such as cardiac arrest, trauma, acute poisoning, and coma, as well as essential emergency techniques. The course emphasizes key aspects including assessment priorities, emergency treatment principles, and nursing measures for acute and critical illnesses. As a comprehensive and practice-oriented discipline, emergency nursing places great importance on the training and performance of emergency skills, while also cultivating students' awareness of emergency situations and their ability to respond effectively.</p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
